{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"5ab2225bd7a9ab22","type":"text","text":"[[DNA]] contains the genetic instructions needed for growth, development and functioning.\n\nStructure is a double helix, which is like a twisted ladder full of [[Nucleotide|nucleotides]].","x":-440,"y":-64,"width":500,"height":144},
		{"id":"953c9cd2dfa39823","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/DNA-1782005969722.webp","x":-547,"y":-703,"width":303,"height":303},
		{"id":"02f91a4ad3dcc3c7","type":"text","text":"![The Structure of DNA](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o_-6JXLYS-k)","x":-140,"y":-722,"width":526,"height":322},
		{"id":"9501899b94b18c78","type":"text","text":"[[RNA|Ribose nucleic acid (RNA)]] is similar to [[DNA]]. But:\n- Single strand structure\n- Bases are: Adenine (A), **Urecil (U)**, Guanine (G) and Cytosine (C)\n- Multiple types","x":680,"y":-661,"width":560,"height":221},
		{"id":"c0b707b170b267c5","type":"text","text":"**mRNA**\n- messager RNA\n- transmits genetic info for a protein to be made","x":2154,"y":-797,"width":547,"height":127},
		{"id":"1230462f02e72f84","type":"text","text":"**rRNA (Ribosome RNA)**\n- Site where instructions are read \n- Info →protein synthesised","x":2154,"y":-502,"width":584,"height":130},
		{"id":"27d737423be38bc7","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/RNA-1782081919436.webp","x":1384,"y":-713,"width":575,"height":324},
		{"id":"b6ff49dd8412291f","type":"text","text":"**tRNA (transfer RNA)**\n- Transports amino acids needed to build a protein","x":2154,"y":-616,"width":469,"height":94},
		{"id":"3b083ddfa523ad89","type":"text","text":"a [[Gene]] is just a specific segment of [[DNA]], which codes instructions for [[Proteins]].","x":460,"y":-25,"width":350,"height":88},
		{"id":"4944b7f6131dfbf6","type":"text","text":"[[Alleles]] are the physical observable characteristic of a gene (e.g. blue, red eyes)","x":1140,"y":-320,"width":440,"height":80},
		{"id":"f6d13f3a2c7a6483","type":"text","text":"[[Mendel's Bean Experiment]] was one of the first gene experiments. Mendel crossbreeded different bean plants to see the results.","x":1061,"y":-38,"width":519,"height":139},
		{"id":"6fbbcd5f6c9a2de6","type":"text","text":"[[Genotype & Phenotype]]","x":1710,"y":71,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"604f417d54b17d1a","type":"text","text":"**Genotype** is the genetic code characteristic.\nExample:\n$BB$ - Homozygous dominant\n$Bb$ - Heterzygous dominant\n$bb$ - Homozygous recessive","x":2227,"y":-240,"width":511,"height":169},
		{"id":"8e7ebfd77005c9be","type":"text","text":"**Phenotype** is the actual observable characteristic.\nExample:\n$BB$ →Brown eyes\n$Bb$ →Brown eyes\n$bb$ →Blue eyes","x":2258,"y":-42,"width":529,"height":160},
		{"id":"e0e85b3bd3f30609","type":"text","text":"[[Punnett Squares]] are a way to visualise the probabilities","x":2360,"y":240,"width":297,"height":75},
		{"id":"38048c546269216c","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Punnett Squares-1783215063453.webp","x":2787,"y":182,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"612a605f4422619a","type":"text","text":"**Pyrimidines**\n- Single ring structure\n- Thymine and Cytosine\n- Remember by the ‘y’","x":-3000,"y":-1169,"width":400,"height":203},
		{"id":"b474c3757594a401","type":"text","text":"**Purines**\n- Double ring structure\n- Adenine and Guanine","x":-3000,"y":-868,"width":364,"height":123},
		{"id":"ebce1f32705471de","type":"text","text":"![[The Structure of DNA-1782005069214.webp]]","x":-2636,"y":-657,"width":624,"height":520},
		{"id":"4bcdb9043fbd697c","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Pasted image 20260705111724.png","x":-2474,"y":-1068,"width":400,"height":323},
		{"id":"36db679b9b165e5e","type":"text","text":"**Base pairing** is when Adenine and Thymine pair up and Guanine and Cytosine pair up. This is kept constant.","x":-2274,"y":-1256,"width":354,"height":100},
		{"id":"3ea81ff0f5084e2f","type":"text","text":"During cellular divison, [[DNA]] condenses into [[Chromosomes]]. Which are x-like structures containing densely wrapped DNA.","x":-1780,"y":-257,"width":389,"height":120},
		{"id":"e37c44f9984cc4f8","type":"text","text":"A [[Nucleotide]] contains:\n- A sugar (deoxyribose, meaning no hydroxyl group)\n- Phosphate group\n- Nitrogenous base (Adenine, Thymine, Cytosine, Guanine)","x":-1764,"y":-593,"width":529,"height":160},
		{"id":"a9f07a5ede766e53","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Chromosones-1782018976213.webp","x":-1260,"y":-257,"width":400,"height":320},
		{"id":"b821792a8f80b1ae","type":"file","file":"01 Resources/Attachments/Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)-1783213434023.webp","x":-2440,"y":207,"width":691,"height":363},
		{"id":"ce8500488d2c40ca","type":"text","text":"[[Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)]] is a laboratory method to create a large amount of copies for a small fragment of DNA. \n### 1. Denature\nHeat DNA sample to near boiling, separating the double helix\n### 2. Annealing\nCool sample, primers attach to specific spots of the DNA strand\n### 3. Elongation \n\nTAQ/DNA Polymerase binds to a certain primer, begins DNA replication in a 5’ to 3’ direction","x":-1620,"y":234,"width":631,"height":492},
		{"id":"0bc7cb7aa56ed4ca","type":"text","text":"**New Technologies**","x":-860,"y":460,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"3a3bec83e618592a","type":"text","text":"**Essays**","x":499,"y":619,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"87626df0fc51e695","type":"text","text":"[[How are proteins made from DNA?]]\n\nCovers:\n- What is a protein\n- How it is synthesised from genetic information","x":957,"y":500,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"365b7d04972f8daa","type":"text","text":"[[DNA and Cellular Division - How are they related?]]","x":929,"y":631,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"df1aea5e7f476189","type":"text","text":"**Cells**","x":-1420,"y":-1600,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6d0b3aa270971ba1","type":"text","text":"[[Prokaryotic]] cells are simpler, genetic info is just floating around","x":-1840,"y":-1540,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"53885418f12d08c4","type":"text","text":"[[Eukaryotic]] cells are complicated, typically in animals, plants.\n\nContain membrane-bound organelles","x":-1820,"y":-1740,"width":291,"height":68},
		{"id":"2869e2dcb1b19330","type":"text","text":"[[Meiosis]] is a cellular division where a non-somatic cell (that is, only sex cells) divides into four genetically different [[Gametes]] cells","x":-2283,"y":-1730,"width":343,"height":90},
		{"id":"d392603d05e23b80","type":"text","text":"[[Mitosis]] is when a somatic cell splits into 2 genetically identical daughter cells.","x":-2289,"y":-1618,"width":289,"height":98},
		{"id":"d411669f27911fb0","x":1179,"y":227,"width":334,"height":103,"type":"text","text":"**Important note:**\nNot all genes code for proteins"},
		{"id":"4b50c47562221a44","type":"text","text":"![[Genes-1782002289530.webp]]","x":400,"y":200,"width":597,"height":300}
	],
